# own_native.tkr — the HOST configuration of the own-backend corpus channel.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL (0.3.1 channel ruling, owner 2026-07-26). These scenarios used to live
# in regressor.tkr as `Given source` rows naming ONE case file, and the runner's snippet FOLD gave
# them one synthesized build per (source, configuration). That fold could never take a
# `Then object well-formed` row, so a cross half of the corpus paid a SECOND build for the very same
# program. Making the corpus a PROJECT removes the fold from the picture entirely: a project `.tkr`
# is compiled at most once (`RegrBuilt`), every scenario of this file runs off that one build, and an
# object check inspects THAT build's own `.o`.
#
# ONE FILE PER PROJECT CONFIGURATION, and that is not a stylistic choice: the project cache is keyed
# on the build SHAPE, not on the env, so the first scenario reaching it fixes the configuration for
# the whole file. A second PROJECT target would therefore need a second `.tkr`, never a second
# `Examples` row — a row that declared a different target would silently assert this file's HOST
# artifact. There is exactly one such file today: the second configuration that ever needed one
# left the compiler when its target did (owner ruling, .31).
#
# The rows that name a `Given source` case file instead are each a lone row of its own cross target or
# of a deliberate PANIC, so a project build would buy nothing and a one-line program is the honest
# minimum for "does the emitter produce a well-formed object for that target" / "does that guard fire".
#
# HOW A ROW ADDRESSES ITS CASE (owner ruling 2026-07-30 — "quer endereçar testes por saída e não pelo
# que deveriam fazer"). Every project row used to read `Then exit = 42`, and `main.tks` encoded WHICH
# case had failed in the exit code: 129 cases over 129 distinct `N`, ten of them above 255. That does
# not merely run out of room, it ALIASES IN SILENCE — `exit(260)` is observed as 4, the code case 4
# already owned, and `exit(256)` is observed as 0, i.e. a failing corpus reporting success.
#
# So the MECHANISM changed, not the range. `main.tks` names each case and asserts its own value with
# `teko::assert::eq_i64` (whose failure quotes the expected and the got value), then announces
# `scenario <name>: ok`; the row demands exactly that line. A failure now names itself in both files
# and needs no lookup table.
#
# THE COUNT MOVED AND THE BUILDS DID NOT. 89 project rows became 130: the 41 probes that existed in
# `main.tks` with no row of their own — the only thing asserting them was the shared `exit = 42`, which
# distinguished nothing — each got one. It is still ONE build for the whole file (`RegrBuilt`), so the
# ten-builds-per-host arithmetic above is untouched; what grew is how many of them are addressable.
#
# THE TWO FILES MOVE TOGETHER, and that is enforced, not hoped for:
# `src/build/own_native_pairing_test.tkt` reads both and fails when a name lives on only one side.
#
# THE EXIT CODE IS NO LONGER A DATA CHANNEL (owner ruling 2026-08-02 — "removi dos testes os exit; os
# regressivos que esperam um exit devem olhar para o stdout, e os codigos que faziam exit agora fazem
# println"). Every case whose exit code merely CARRIED a value — the cross-target verticals and the
# null-arm C-route oracle — now `println`s that value and its row asserts it on stdout. The exit code
# stays the observable of ONLY two kinds of program: a deliberate PANIC (`Then it traps`, non-zero via
# stderr) and the lone KNOWN-STOP whose whole subject IS the exit syscall firing in program mode
# (`capture_off_in_program_mode_still_exits_for_real`) — neither of which a stdout line can express.
