Feature: F9 romaneio .31 — the audit's positive regressions (backend x scenario)

  # (B-S1) The own backend coerces into a null-union at EVERY placement that declares a type, not
  # only at the binding: `x = null` after a `T | null` binding used to write a raw `const 0` into the
  # VReg the `match` then reads as the wrapper's base address — a load from address 0. The arc that
  # used to prove it — the C backend, which wrapped at the assign all along — is gone with the C
  # emitter, so what proves it now is this scenario's own asserted exit.
  Scenario: null_union_reassign_to_null (own-native — o cenario afirma o proprio valor; nao ha mais arco C contra o que diferir)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario null_union_reassign_to_null: ok"


Feature: class layout — a class registers the layout of its effective instance fields

  # The native lowering collected a layout for a `StructBody` only; a `ClassBody` fell through the
  # catch-all arm of `collect_struct_layout_decl` and registered NOTHING, so EVERY class
  # instantiation died at compile time with "no layout registered for struct `…`" — one class in
  # one namespace, with no same-name type anywhere, was enough to trigger it.
  #
  # `class_layout_effective_fields` asserts both halves off this channel's ONE build (`corpus.tks`,
  # `f_class_layout_effective_fields`). The class half: an `abstract` base's fields are the PREFIX
  # of the derived instance (field-flattening composition), so the inherited AND the own fields must
  # each read back the value they were constructed with, and a method summing across the boundary
  # must agree — an overlapping or reordered slot shows up as a wrong exit code, not as a compile
  # error. The struct half is the control: it is the arm that already worked, so a fix that broke it
  # fails HERE instead of passing quietly.
  #
  # IT IS A MEMBER OF THIS CHANNEL, not a `Given source` row of its own: the defect is in the NATIVE
  # LOWERING's layout collector, which is exactly what this channel's program exercises, and a member
  # costs no build (0.3.1 channel ruling).
  Scenario: class_layout_effective_fields (own-native — a class instance is a field-shaped object)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario class_layout_effective_fields: ok"


Feature: list growth builtin — the native backend grows a list through the runtime

  # The own backend had NO lowering for the checker's generic growth-builtin family
  # (`teko::list::empty`, `teko::list::push`): `call_symbol` honest-stopped on
  # every one of them, so no program that accumulates into a list could be built at all — the
  # compiler itself first among them. They now lower to the SAME runtime entry the retired C
  # emitter targeted (`tk_slice_push`), with the item passed by address and the
  # grown length returned through an out-parameter slot.
  #
  # Every row below is a MEMBER of this channel (one build, no extra cost) and asserts its own exit
  # code: a wrong `sizeof`, a swapped argument order, or a length read out of the wrong slot all show
  # up as a wrong exit, never as a compile error.
  Scenario: list_push_scalar_loop (own-native — the accumulation the whole compiler is written in)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario list_push_scalar_loop: ok"

  Scenario: list_push_value_semantics (own-native — a push returns a new list and never mutates its base)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario list_push_value_semantics: ok"

  Scenario: list_push_bool_and_enum (own-native — the one-byte and enum-carrier element classes)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario list_push_bool_and_enum: ok"

  Scenario: list_empty_has_no_elements (own-native — the (null, 0) pair needs no runtime call)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario list_empty_has_no_elements: ok"


Feature: short-circuit logical operators — the native backend branches instead of computing both operands

  # `&&`/`||` had NO lowering in the native backend: `lower_binary` sent every binary node to
  # `lbinop_of`, whose two-way float/integer split routed a `bool`-typed logical operator into the
  # INTEGER opcode table, where it died in the `_ =>` arm as "integer operator not yet lowered". The
  # message named the wrong family and the wrong kind of gap — what was missing is a control-flow
  # shape, not an opcode. Every program with a logical operator in it was unbuildable natively, the
  # compiler's own source included (`needs_lr_save`, two lines long, was the stop).
  #
  # The row is a MEMBER of this channel (one build, no extra cost). It asserts the full truth table
  # AND the short-circuit guarantee: two of its cases have a divide-by-zero as their RIGHT operand,
  # so a lowering that evaluates both sides — which a bitwise `IAnd`/`IOr` stand-in would have done —
  # traps and kills the process instead of returning a wrong exit code.
  Scenario: short_circuit_and_or (own-native — the right operand runs only when the left did not decide)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario short_circuit_and_or: ok"


Feature: aggregate slice elements — the native backend boxes a pushed aggregate into fresh per-execution storage

  # (0.3.1.0 degrau 4 — NATIVE-AGG-SLICE-BY-ADDRESS) The native backend holds a `[]struct` as a slice
  # of ADDRESSES, and its value for a struct/class instance is the address of an `alloca` — ONE frame
  # slot per INSTRUCTION. A push that stored that address as-is would store the SAME address every
  # iteration of a loop, so the whole list would read back as N copies of the last item; the backend
  # honest-stopped on it instead, which made every aggregate accumulation unbuildable — the compiler's
  # own source (`[]EncWord`, `[]LInst`, `[]MInst`, …) first among them.
  #
  # The push now copies the aggregate into fresh root-arena storage (`tk_slice_elem_box`) and pushes
  # THAT address. The rows are MEMBERS of this channel (one build, no extra cost) and each asserts its
  # own exit: the loop row fails with the aliasing bug because its elements read back equal, and the
  # copy row fails because a mutation of the source binding after the push reaches the element.
  #
  # STILL OPEN BY DECISION (owner 2026-07-28): assignment out of such a slice (`let a = ps[0]`) is an
  # ALIAS, not a copy — the by-value element model is its own lane. Do not let a scenario here claim it.
  Scenario: push_aggregate_in_loop (own-native — one distinct element per iteration)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario push_aggregate_in_loop: ok"

  Scenario: push_aggregate_copies_the_item (own-native — the pushed element is a copy, not the binding)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario push_aggregate_copies_the_item: ok"

  Scenario: push_class_in_loop (own-native — a class instance is boxed by the same layout width)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario push_class_in_loop: ok"


Feature: fat match-value — a `str`/`[]T`-valued match is a fat-pointer producer

  # (0.3.1.0 degrau 5) `lower_fat_expr` accepted a CLOSED set of fat producers — string literal,
  # array literal, bound local, struct field, `if`-expression, direct call — and a `match`-VALUE was
  # not in it, so every `str`-returning function whose body is a match honest-stopped. The arm now
  # exists (`lower_match_fat`): the same linear test chain the scalar match-value builds, merging TWO
  # block-args (ptr, len) instead of one, with the reassigned scalars following the pair.
  #
  # The row is a MEMBER of this channel (one build, no extra cost) and covers all three halves the
  # merge can get wrong: the `str` shape, the `[]T` shape (whose LENGTH differs per arm, so a merge
  # that carried a sibling's length is visible), and an arm that also re-assigns an enclosing scalar
  # (which rides the merge AFTER the pair).
  Scenario: fat_match_value (own-native — the match-expression fat producer)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario fat_match_value: ok"


Feature: variant fat member — a `str`/`[]T` case keeps its LENGTH across the wrapper

  # (0.3.1.0 degrau 6) The own backend's variant/optional wrapper was `{ i32 tag; ONE payload word }`
  # sized per CONSTRUCTION, so a `str`/`[]T` member's LENGTH was dropped where it was built. The stop
  # that surfaced it — `slice match pattern not yet lowered` — was in the PATTERN, and giving that
  # pattern its arm alone would have bound an `xs` whose `.len` reads whatever sits past the pointer:
  # it would have compiled and miscompiled in silence, degrau 3's trap wearing degrau 1's clothes.
  #
  # The owner's ruling of 2026-07-28: a UNIFORM 24-byte `{ tag@0; ptr@8; len@16 }` for every variant
  # and every case — one shape, one set of offsets, 8 bytes wasted on every case that has no fat
  # member, and no case whose offsets differ from its siblings'.
  #
  # The row asserts LENGTHS, per call and per case, because a length is the half a lossy construction
  # cannot supply by luck; the contents are walked through those lengths so a plausible length with
  # the wrong pointer is caught too.
  Scenario: variant_fat_member (own-native — the uniform 24-byte wrapper keeps a fat member's length)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario variant_fat_member: ok"


Feature: str equality — `str == str` has a native lowering

  # (0.3.1.0 degrau 7) `lower_compare` asked `prim_kind_of` for its operands' ONE machine kind, and a
  # `str` has none: it is two words. Every string comparison in the compiler's own source therefore
  # honest-stopped ("`str` has no single PrimKind, asked by the comparison chain"), starting with
  # `teko::backend::name_in_symbols`'s `syms[i].name == name`.
  #
  # The arm calls the runtime's OWN `tk_str_eq` with the two ABI-flattened (ptr, len) pairs — the
  # same flattening every fat argument already gets — and normalizes the C `bool` return through its
  # LOW BYTE, because the bits above it are undefined and testing the whole register answered "equal"
  # for two strings that differ.
  Scenario: str_equality (own-native — string equality through the runtime's tk_str_eq)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario str_equality: ok"


Feature: declared variant match arms — a `type X = variant …` subject selects the arm its own case names

  # THE SILENT ONE. A declared variant reaches the native lowering as a nominal `Named` (the checker
  # keeps user types nominal), while every union-shaped decision in that lowering matched on
  # `checker::Variant` — the shape an INLINE `A | B` arrives as. Both halves of the union protocol
  # degenerated at once: the value was constructed with NO tag+payload wrapper, and every bind
  # pattern answered "always matches", so the FIRST arm swallowed every subject. The two wrongs
  # cancel on arm one (the whole-value bind reads the unwrapped payload at offset 0) and produce a
  # WRONG ANSWER on every other arm — no stop, no diagnostic, a `Square` running the `Circle` arm.
  #
  # It is the one defect class a green fixpoint cannot catch: two equally wrong compilers emit
  # identical bytes and the gate approves. The corpus is the only guard there is, so these rows
  # assert the BOUND VALUE and not merely which arm ran — an always-true test would pass a
  # which-arm check for the first case and lie about the rest — and they enter the FIRST, a MIDDLE
  # and the LAST arm of a three-case variant.
  Scenario: declared_variant_arms (own-native — each case runs its own arm, with its own payload)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario declared_variant_arms: ok"

  # The spellings that resolve a case through their OWN path: the match-VALUE (which merges its arms
  # through a block argument), the BINDINGLESS case, and the FIELD destructure — the last of which
  # did not even compile ("field match pattern over a non-variant subject") before a declared variant
  # looked like a variant.
  Scenario: declared_variant_match_value (own-native — value, bindingless and field spellings pick the same case)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario declared_variant_match_value: ok"

  # The NON-NOMINAL cases beside the nominal ones: the built-in `error` member of a declared variant
  # (resolved by keyword, not by name — a table that dropped it would mis-tag every case after it)
  # and the `null` case of a union. Both are entered by their own value while the nominal cases keep
  # their own arms.
  Scenario: declared_variant_error_and_null (own-native — the error case and the null case, mixed with nominals)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario declared_variant_error_and_null: ok"


Feature: string interpolation in a FAT position — a `TInterp` is its own fat-pointer producer

  # (0.3.1.0 degrau 9) `lower_fat_expr` accepted a CLOSED producer set (literal, array literal,
  # bound local, struct field, `if`-expression, `match`-value, direct call) and a string
  # interpolation was not in it, so a `$"..."` reaching a FAT position (a call argument, a
  # loop-carried reassignment, …) honest-stopped — starting with the compiler's own
  # `ar_header`/`ar_pad_right` (`src/backend/objfile_ar.tks`), whose `ar_pad_right($"{size}", …)`
  # passes an interpolation as a call argument. The arm now chains the runtime's
  # out-parameter-length twins (`tk_str_concat_len`/`tk_i64_to_str_len`/`tk_u64_to_str_len`,
  # `teko_rt.h`) — added because this backend's own `LCall` captures only ONE result register,
  # one short of the two-eightbyte register pair `tk_str_concat`'s REAL C ABI struct return
  # occupies; the pre-degrau-9 lowering had called that same runtime symbol with only ONE word per
  # `tk_str` operand, which is not a missing length so much as a wrong ABI call.
  #
  # The SAME gap sat behind `teko::str::concat`/`teko::string::concat` (the LEGISLATED
  # string-building builtin `ar_pad_right`'s own loop calls, and the primitive the rest of this
  # project's own source is written in by the hundreds): `call_symbol` had no entry for it either,
  # so a call reached would have silently mangled to a symbol nothing defines — a LINK failure,
  # not a named stop. `lower_concat_call` folds the builtin's own N literal `str` arguments
  # pairwise through the SAME out-parameter-length concat.
  #
  # The row is a MEMBER of this channel (one build, no extra cost) and covers every FAT position
  # this degrau closed: a call argument, a loop-carried self-reassignment (the minimal
  # reproduction), a multi-hole/multi-piece interpolation checked by both content and length, and
  # the `concat` builtin folded over more than two arguments.
  Scenario: interp_fat_positions (own-native — interpolation and `concat` in every fat position)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario interp_fat_positions: ok"


Feature: str query/slice builtins — `contains`/`ends_with`/`slice`/`slice_to`/`slice_from` have a native lowering

  # (0.3.1.0 degrau 11 / raiz-comum §R5) `call_symbol` had no resolution for any of these five bare
  # names, so a reached call fell through `unresolved_builtin_stop` and honest-stopped ("builtin
  # `contains` not yet lowered") — first surfaced through `teko::assert::str_contains`'s own body
  # (`src/assert/assert.tks`), which every `.tkt` test file in this project calls via the injected
  # assert seed. `native_builtin_symbol` now resolves all five, split by RETURN SHAPE:
  # `contains`/`ends_with` are `(str, str): bool` (`builtin_str_query_symbol`) and need no
  # out-parameter-length twin — a `bool` fits the single result register every scalar call already
  # captures. `slice`/`slice_to`/`slice_from` are `str`-returning (`builtin_str_slice_symbol`) and
  # resolve to the runtime's out-parameter-length twins (`tk_str_slice_len`/`tk_str_slice_to_len`/
  # `tk_str_slice_from_len`, `teko_rt.h`) through the SAME `lower_len_out_call` shape 0.3.1.0
  # degrau 9 built for `tk_str_concat_len` — `lower_call_fat`'s own generic tail already appends
  # the hidden length out-slot once the symbol resolves, so no new lowering ARM was needed.
  #
  # The two rows are MEMBERS of this channel (one build, no extra cost): one exercises the
  # predicate pair (a match, a miss, and the empty-needle/empty-suffix edge each runtime function
  # documents as trivially true), the other exercises all three slice builtins by both LENGTH and
  # CONTENT, so a plausible length with the wrong bytes is caught too.
  Scenario: str_contains_and_ends_with (own-native — the two `str` predicate builtins)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario str_contains_and_ends_with: ok"

