Feature: array-literal return escape — a `[]T` array literal returned directly by a function

  # (0.3.1.0 array-literal-escape) the native backend built an array literal's backing bytes in an
  # `alloca` — the CALLEE's own frame slot — and handed the caller that bare address as the
  # literal's fat-pointer ptr half. A function `return`ing a fresh array literal therefore handed
  # its caller a pointer into a frame about to unwind: a SECOND such call, issued before the FIRST
  # call's result was ever read, reused that same stack memory, and the first result silently read
  # back the second call's bytes — no crash, no stop, the wrong answer. The C emitter never had
  # this bug (its array-literal fast path always allocates through `malloc`); the fix boxes the
  # native backend's filled buffer into fresh per-execution runtime storage before handing back its
  # address, matching the C route unconditionally rather than only on the return edge.
  #
  # The row exercises the scalar (`[]i64`) element case, the FAT (`[]str`) element case, and the
  # empty literal, each pair of producer calls issued before either result is read.
  Scenario: array_lit_return_escape (own-native — two array-literal-returning calls, first result read after the second call)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario array_lit_return_escape: ok"


Feature: union placement — a value written into a union-typed slot is wrapped, whatever the slot is

  # (0.3.1.0 degrau 25) every own-backend placement coerces its value into the slot's DECLARED type
  # (`lower_value_into_type`) — except two, which had no declared type to coerce against: a
  # struct-literal field (the layout table keeps machine classes, not checker types) and a call
  # argument. A bare `null` in a struct field therefore stopped the whole build; a bare `T` in
  # either placement compiled and wrote its own raw representation into a slot a later `match` reads
  # as the wrapper's BASE ADDRESS, which SIGSEGV'd against a C route that answered correctly.
  # `collect_field_decls` (the checker-typed companion of the layout table) and
  # `callee_param_types` (off `TCall.callee_type`) are what those two placements now read.
  #
  # The first row exercises a scalar union field, a FAT (`str`) union field read back through both
  # its length and its first byte, a class instance's union field through its factory, and both a
  # bare `null` and a bare scalar at a call argument.
  Scenario: union_placement_field_and_argument (own-native — a struct field, a class field and a call argument, each a union slot)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario union_placement_field_and_argument: ok"

  # The second row is the twin degrau 23 left behind: `lower_variant_construct` got the
  # DIRECT-vs-NESTED split and `lower_null_union_construct` did not, so a bare case placed into a
  # `<variant> | null` slot stored the case's own raw address and the inner `match` read that value's
  # FIRST FIELD as the inner union's tag — no arm taken, no diagnostic, execution falling through the
  # whole `match`. Both inner cases (tags 0 and 1) carry distinct field values so a dropped inner
  # wrapper cannot pass by coincidence.
  Scenario: union_placement_nested_variant (own-native — a bare variant case placed into a `<variant> | null` slot, through both match levels)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario union_placement_nested_variant: ok"

Feature: no cast truncates — a narrowing `to` whose value does not fit its destination PANICS on both routes (0.3.1 aridade numérica, owner ruling 2026-07-29)

  # Owner, literal (2026-07-29): *"Uma rule para cast (implícito ou explícito), não pode truncar, no
  # caso de explícito de tipo maior que outro, deve ser feito em runtime (e panicar se overflow)."*
  #
  # `docs/design/cast-width-hygiene-0.3.1.md` §4.1 asserted this guard was already live "end-to-end".
  # It was live on the C route ONLY: `emit_cast` routes a lossy int→int cast through the runtime's
  # `tk_to_<dst>_<carrier>`, which calls `tk_panic_cast`. The NATIVE route had no equivalent — it
  # emitted a bare `Trunc`, and `Trunc` selects a 32-BIT move on both isels. Measured before the fix,
  # native route, each a silent wrong answer where the C route already panicked or already kept the
  # value:
  #
  #   u64 300  to u8   → 300          (a `u8` holding a number no `u8` represents)
  #   i64 2^40 to i32  → 0            (real overflow, silent)
  #   i64 2^40 to u64  → 0            (equal widths still picked `Trunc`)
  #   i64 -5   to i8   → 4294967291   (the 32-bit move zero-extends where the sign had to live)
  #   i64 -1   to u32  → 4294967295   (a negative accepted by an unsigned destination)
  #
  # These three rows pin the PANIC side; the FIT side (an in-range narrow keeping its exact value,
  # and a widen still costing no guard) is pinned by value inside this channel's own build
  # (`f_cast_narrow_in_range_keeps_value`, `f_cast_narrow_negative_keeps_sign`,
  # `f_cast_same_width_sign_change_keeps_value`, `f_cast_widen_keeps_value`). They need their OWN
  # files because a panic aborts the process and would take every later assertion with it.
  #
  # `Then it traps` + `stderr pattern` rather than an exit code, for the reason `TK_PANIC_MARKER`
  # itself records: an abort's status is synthesised from the wait status by whoever ran the child,
  # so no number distinguishes "the guard fired" from "the program crashed". The pattern names WHICH
  # guard killed it and that the program chose to.
  Scenario: cast_narrow_panics_u64_to_u8 (own-native — 300 does not fit a u8)
    Given source = "cases/cast_narrow_panics_u64_to_u8.tks"
    When built and run
    Then it traps
    And stderr pattern = "teko: deliberate panic: impossible conversion"

  Scenario: cast_narrow_panics_u64_to_u8 (C route — the route that was already right)
    Given source = "cases/cast_narrow_panics_u64_to_u8.tks"
    Given env = ["TEKO_BACKEND=c"]
    When built and run
    Then it traps
    And stderr pattern = "teko: deliberate panic: impossible conversion"

  Scenario: cast_narrow_panics_i64_to_i32 (own-native — 2^40 does not fit an i32)
    Given source = "cases/cast_narrow_panics_i64_to_i32.tks"
    When built and run
    Then it traps
    And stderr pattern = "teko: deliberate panic: impossible conversion"

  Scenario: cast_narrow_panics_i64_to_i32 (C route)
    Given source = "cases/cast_narrow_panics_i64_to_i32.tks"
    Given env = ["TEKO_BACKEND=c"]
