Feature: slice element write — `xs[i] op= v` has a native lowering (0.3.1.0 degrau 28)

  # The native backend's `lower_assign` honest-stopped on `AssignKind::Index` outright ("slice element
  # index-assignment not yet lowered"), which is what broke EVERY leg of this channel: the corpus's own
  # `f_implicit_widen_targets` writes into a slice element because that is how one writes Teko, so the
  # whole project failed to build and every row of this file — `own_arith_exit` first — failed with it.
  # The stop also swallowed a whole loop spelling: the parser desugars `loop v in xs { v = … }` over a
  # `mut` container into exactly this element write ("the ratified element-assignment primitive",
  # `loop_slice_writeback`), so a mutable for-each was unbuildable natively too.
  #
  # The write is now the sibling of the element READ: the receiver lowered as a FAT producer (the
  # LENGTH half is what the bounds guard needs), the index lowered EXACTLY ONCE and shared by the guard
  # and the address, the guard trapping through the SAME positioned `tk_panic_oob_at` the C route's own
  # `emit_index_assign` calls, and the store split by element shape — fat (both halves of the
  # `{ptr, len}` pair), register value (`store_assign_value`, the struct field's own tail, so a
  # compound `op=` loads-computes-stores) and aggregate (boxed into fresh per-execution storage, byte
  # for byte what a pushed aggregate already gets).
  #
  # Every row below is a MEMBER of this channel (one build, no extra cost) and asserts VALUES read back
  # through the element the write named, with the SIBLING elements re-checked: a store that landed one
  # slot away fails here rather than passing on the element it corrupted.
  Scenario: slice_elem_store_boundaries (own-native — index 0, a middle index and the last, over both an array literal's buffer and a push-grown one)
    When built and run
